  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Houser shut them down. The Giants couldn't answer.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what happens when one team's pitching holds and the other team leaves runners in scoring position stranded: eight RISP at-bats yielded one run. The offense had chances. A two-on situation in the second inning alone accounted for three left on base. But Houser's six scoreless innings and the late-inning relief from Winn and Sanmartin kept Houston's deficit at one through seven innings. The game stayed tied into the eighth. Then it broke. The Giants had held the lead from the first inning through the seventh, but the bullpen gap in the ninth. Or more precisely, the offense's inability to add insurance when the pitching was good enough. Became fatal. They wasted what their pitching earned them.",
    "what_to_watch": "Eight left on base is not a failure when the opposition pitches a shutout. It's a waste of a chance to win. The difference between scoring once in eight RISP situations and twice would have been the game. That's the margin they're working with right now. When the pitching is this clean and the baseline is one run, the offense has to find two. They didn't.",
